TITLE: Deadline check cannot fire on runs shorter than 64 pops, and the max_states safeguard is unreachable in practice (O(n^2) coverage scan)
FILE: /workspace/cpachecker/analyses/value_analysis.py:834
SEVERITY CLAIMED: minor
DETAIL: Three coupled issues in the loop guards. (1) `checks` is incremented to 1 on the first iteration and the deadline is only consulted when `(checks & 0x3F) == 0`, so no run with fewer than 64 waitlist pops ever checks the clock: with an already-expired deadline, simple_safe.c (6 pops) and loop_safe.c (47 pops) both return `timed_out=False, exhausted=True`, i.e. a definite verdict for a run that should have been reported as UNKNOWN; the overshoot after the last check is also unbounded in wall-clock terms because per-iteration cost grows with the reached set. (2) `run_value_analysis`'s `timelimit` parameter is `Optional`, and `CPAAlgorithm` leaves `self.deadline = None` when both `deadline` and `timelimit` are None, so a caller passing None gets no wall-clock bound at all. (3) The only remaining bound is `max_states=400_000` (line 898, which also permits max_states+1 states because the test is `>` after the add), but coverage is a linear scan of the whole location partition in `CompositeStopOperator.stop`, making exploration quadratic: measured 6145 / 8577 / 12097 reached states for 5 / 10 / 20 s on the repro below, i.e. reaching 400_000 states would take on the order of 6 hours. Coverage does not guarantee termination for this domain (unbounded integer values with an undecidable loop guard), so the timelimit is doing all the work and the state limit is effectively decorative.
CLAIMED REPRO: # (1) expired deadline is never observed on a short run:
